<h1>Part 1 &middot; The courses under a program, with no Zesty relational field</h1>
<p class="lede">Program <b>Basketball</b>. The data is Salesforce&rsquo;s records, one file per tier, with Traction Rec&rsquo;s fields at the <b>root</b> of each row. Each course already carries <code>TREX1__Program__c</code> &mdash; the relationship was never missing, it just had to be reachable in one request.</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>Approach</th><th class="n">Requests</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
  <tr class="win"><td>1 &middot; JavaScript filters the tier file</td><td class="n">1</td><td>what the catalog components already do</td></tr>
  <tr><td>2 &middot; Zesty relational field (today)</td><td class="n">0</td><td>the field we are removing</td></tr>
  <tr class="win"><td>3 &middot; Parsley <code>api.json.get</code> on the synced file</td><td class="n">1</td><td><b>server-rendered</b> &mdash; works since the files changed shape (&sect;0)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>4 &middot; Class&rsquo;s nested fields</td><td class="n">1</td><td>read in JavaScript (decided 2026-10-02)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>6 &middot; Native <code>each</code> + per-item <code>api.json.get</code></td><td class="n">119</td><td>works, but one fetch per row &mdash; 71&nbsp;s for every course</td></tr>
  <tr class="win"><td>7 &middot; Parent &rarr; children file</td><td class="n">1</td><td><b>a page reads only its own children</b> &mdash; small, no filter (&sect;7)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="card">
  <h2>0 &middot; Why the synced files changed shape <span class="badge b-warn">2026-09-30</span></h2>
  <p class="why">Until 30 Sep every file wrapped the record: <code>&#123; meta, data &#125;</code>. Parsley could fetch those files and loop them, but reading any field <b>crashed the page</b>. We isolated why on small fixtures, one probe per render (a crash takes the whole page down):</p>
  <table style="margin:0 0 14px">
  <tr><th>Probe inside <code>each api.json.get(&hellip;) as r</code></th><th>Result</th></tr>
  <tr><td>a top-level value, on a row that ALSO holds nested objects &mdash; <code>r.Id</code></td><td style="background:#e7f5ee">&#9989; renders</td></tr>
  <tr><td>a dotted read into a nested object &mdash; <code>r.data.Name</code></td><td style="background:#fdeaea">&#10060; page crash</td></tr>
  <tr><td>printing a nested object &mdash; <code>r.data</code></td><td style="background:#fdeaea">&#10060; page crash</td></tr>
  <tr><td>printing a nested array &mdash; <code>r.list</code></td><td style="background:#fff6e0">&#9888; the literal word <code>Array</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td>looping a nested object &mdash; <code>each r.data as d</code></td><td style="background:#fff6e0">&#9888; zero passes, no error</td></tr>
  <tr><td>an object root instead of an array root</td><td style="background:#e7f5ee">&#9989; one pass, top-level values render</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="why"><b>Conclusion:</b> Parsley binds each fetched row one level deep &mdash; a flat map of key &rarr; value. Nested values are harmless while nothing reads them, but nothing below the root is reachable. The old files had <b>no</b> top-level values at all: every field Salesforce returned sat one level down, inside <code>data</code>.</p>
  <p class="why"><b>So we removed the envelope, not the data.</b> Salesforce&rsquo;s fields moved to the root, unchanged in name and value; the sync&rsquo;s own bookkeeping stays nested under <code>meta</code>, where nobody needs Parsley to read it.</p>
<pre>before  &#123; "meta": &#123; "hash": &hellip; &#125;, "data": &#123; "Id": "a1za&hellip;", "Name": "Adult Basketball League", "TREX1__Program__c": "a31a&hellip;" &#125; &#125;
after   &#123; "Id": "a1za&hellip;", "Name": "Adult Basketball League", "TREX1__Program__c": "a31a&hellip;", "meta": &#123; "hash": &hellip; &#125; &#125;</pre>
  <div class="note"><b>Why not a second, flat copy of every file?</b> That was the earlier plan (<code>course.flat.json</code>). One set of files is simpler, the cron keeps it current with no extra step, and three of the four tiers are already completely flat once the envelope goes. The flat copy of courses has been deleted. Only <code>class</code> still nests &mdash; see &sect;4.</div>
</div>

<div class="card best">
  <h2>1 &middot; JavaScript, one fetch <span class="badge b-ok">client-rendered</span></h2>
  <p class="why">No Zesty field of any kind beyond <code>tractionrec_id</code>. No index, no per-parent file, no parent column. The parent is Salesforce&rsquo;s own <code>TREX1__Program__c</code>, read straight off the record.</p>
<pre>fetch("/tractionrec/courses.json")
  .then(r =&gt; r.json())
  .then(rows =&gt; rows.filter(r =&gt; r.TREX1__Program__c === programTrId));</pre>
  <div class="out"><span class="out-label">Live output &mdash; rendered in your browser</span>
    <div id="kroc-js-out">loading&hellip;</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h2>2 &middot; Zesty relational field <span class="badge b-warn">control</span></h2>
  <p class="why">Today&rsquo;s approach, shown so the others can be compared. It holds a Zesty ZUID, is written once at create, and never follows a re-parent in Traction Rec.</p>
<pre class="run">&#123;&#123;each courses as zc where FIND_IN_SET('7-feeac0fae9-28d0cs', zc.programs) sort by zc.course_name asc&#125;&#125;
  &#123;&#123;zc.course_name&#125;&#125;
&#123;&#123;end-each&#125;&#125;</pre>
  <div class="out"><span class="out-label">Output &mdash; rendered server-side by Parsley</span>
  {{each courses as zc where FIND_IN_SET('7-feeac0fae9-28d0cs', zc.programs) sort by zc.course_name asc}}<b>{{!zc.course_name}}</b> &middot; {{end-each}}
  </div>
</div>

<div class="card best">
  <h2>3 &middot; Parsley <code>api.json.get</code>, on the synced file <span class="badge b-ok">server-rendered</span></h2>
  <p class="why">The same relationship, rendered by Parsley from the <b>same</b> file &sect;1 reads. Before &sect;0&rsquo;s change this crashed the page; now it is an ordinary loop.</p>
<pre class="run">&#123;&#123;each api.json.get(https://kroc.zesty.dev/tractionrec/courses.json) as c&#125;&#125;
  &#123;&#123;if &#123;c.TREX1__Program__c&#125; == (a31a6000000EhmvAAC)&#125;&#125;
    &#123;&#123;c.Name&#125;&#125;
  &#123;&#123;end-if&#125;&#125;
&#123;&#123;end-each&#125;&#125;</pre>
  <div class="out"><span class="out-label">Output &mdash; server-rendered, same 8 courses as &sect;1 and &sect;2</span>
  {{each api.json.get(https://kroc.zesty.dev/tractionrec/courses.json) as c}}{{if {c.TREX1__Program__c} == (a31a6000000EhmvAAC)}}<b>{{!c.Name}}</b> &middot; {{end-if}}{{end-each}}
  </div>
  <div class="out" style="margin-top:12px"><span class="out-label">Per-item files work the same way &mdash; /tractionrec/courses/a1za60000026CewAAE.json</span>
  {{each api.json.get(https://kroc.zesty.dev/tractionrec/courses/a1za60000026CewAAE.json) as r}}<b>{{!r.Name}}</b> &middot; program <code>{{!r.TREX1__Program__c}}</code>{{end-each}}
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><b><code>include</code> still works too</b>, and is still different: it puts the file&rsquo;s raw JSON into the HTML &mdash; a server-rendered <i>payload</i> to hand to JavaScript, not content a crawler reads.<br><code>&#123;&#123; include /tractionrec/courses/&#123;this.tractionrec_id&#125;.json &#125;&#125;</code></div>
  <div class="out"><span class="out-label">include, on the same per-item file</span>
  <textarea readonly class="raw" aria-label="include output, raw text">{{ include /tractionrec/courses/a1za60000026CewAAE.json }}</textarea>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h2>4 &middot; What is still out of Parsley&rsquo;s reach: Class <span class="badge b-warn">nested by Salesforce</span></h2>
  <p class="why">Removing the envelope made every field readable on <code>program_category</code>, <code>program</code> and <code>course</code>. <code>class</code> is different: most of what a class card shows lives inside Salesforce&rsquo;s own relationship objects, <code>TREX1__Course_Option__r</code> and <code>TREX1__cso_Course_Session__r</code>, which is how the query returns them. That nesting is Traction Rec&rsquo;s structure, so the sync keeps it &mdash; and Parsley cannot reach it.</p>
  <table style="margin:0 0 14px">
  <tr><th>From <code>classes.json</code></th><th>Parsley</th><th>JavaScript</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Id</code>, <code>Deep_Link_URL__c</code>, <code>TREX1__Course_Option__c</code> &hellip; (root)</td><td>&#9989;</td><td>&#9989;</td></tr>
  <tr><td>name, dates, days, ages, capacity &mdash; under <code>TREX1__Course_Option__r</code></td><td>&#10060;</td><td>&#9989;</td></tr>
  <tr><td>the parent course &mdash; <code>TREX1__cso_Course_Session__r.TREX1__Course__c</code></td><td>&#10060; so no course&rarr;class filter</td><td>&#9989;</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>prices</code> (array) &middot; Program&rsquo;s <code>TREX1__Program_Category__c</code> (array)</td><td>&#10060;</td><td>&#9989;</td></tr>
  </table>
  <div class="out"><span class="out-label">Root fields of a class, from classes.json &mdash; server-rendered</span>
  {{each api.json.get(https://kroc.zesty.dev/tractionrec/classes.json) as cl}}{{if {cl.Id} == (a1ua600000p4VMQAA2)}}<code>{{!cl.Id}}</code> &middot; option <code>{{!cl.TREX1__Course_Option__c}}</code> &middot; <a href="{{!cl.Deep_Link_URL__c}}">register</a>{{end-if}}{{end-each}}
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><b>Decided 2026-10-02: nested class fields are read in JavaScript</b>, not flattened for Parsley. The sync keeps Traction Rec&rsquo;s structure, and &sect;7 shows a course&rsquo;s classes read that way. The earlier hand-published <code>class.flat.json</code> demo is gone.</div>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h2>5 &middot; A detail page, server-rendered by Parsley <span class="badge b-ok">1 fetch</span></h2>
  <p class="why">A detail page needs no file of its own shape: it fetches the tier file and keeps the row whose <code>Id</code> matches the item&rsquo;s <code>tractionrec_id</code>. Below is a course page&rsquo;s body, rendered from <code>courses.json</code> &mdash; real HTML a crawler reads, not an embedded JSON blob.</p>
<pre class="run">&#123;&#123;each api.json.get(https://kroc.zesty.dev/tractionrec/courses.json) as c&#125;&#125;
  &#123;&#123;if &#123;c.Id&#125; == (a1za60000026CewAAE)&#125;&#125;
    &lt;h3&gt;&#123;&#123;c.Name&#125;&#125;&lt;/h3&gt; &#123;&#123;c.TREX1__Rich_Description__c&#125;&#125;
  &#123;&#123;end-if&#125;&#125;
&#123;&#123;end-each&#125;&#125;</pre>
  <div class="out"><span class="out-label">Output &mdash; course a1za60000026CewAAE</span>
  {{each api.json.get(https://kroc.zesty.dev/tractionrec/courses.json) as c}}{{if {c.Id} == (a1za60000026CewAAE)}}<h3 style="margin:0 0 6px">{{!c.Name}}</h3>{{!c.TREX1__Rich_Description__c}}{{end-if}}{{end-each}}
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><b>On a real course page the literal id is <code>(&#123;this.tractionrec_id&#125;)</code></b>; it is written out here so the runner can execute it, since this page&rsquo;s own item has no <code>tractionrec_id</code>. <b>Those parentheses are safe only because Salesforce ids contain no hyphen.</b> Against a Zesty ZUID the same form silently matches nothing (Part&nbsp;2 &sect;B).</div>
  <table style="margin-top:16px">
  <tr><th>file</th><th><code>include</code></th><th><code>api.json.get</code> root field</th><th><code>api.json.get</code> nested field</th></tr>
  <tr><td>per-item (<code>/tractionrec/courses/&lt;id&gt;.json</code>)</td><td>&#9989; as text</td><td>&#9989;</td><td>&#10060; crash</td></tr>
  <tr><td>tier (<code>/tractionrec/courses.json</code>)</td><td>&#9989; as text</td><td>&#9989;</td><td>&#10060; crash</td></tr>
  <tr><td>before 30 Sep, either layout</td><td>&#9989; as text</td><td>&#10060; there were none</td><td>&#10060; crash</td></tr>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h2>6 &middot; Native <code>each</code> + <code>api.json.get</code> on each course&rsquo;s own file <span class="badge b-warn">works &middot; one fetch per row</span></h2>
  <p class="why">The earlier test, restored against the real per-item files. Loop Zesty&rsquo;s own <code>courses</code> model, and inside each row fetch that course&rsquo;s Traction Rec file by its <code>tractionrec_id</code>. A <b>loop alias does interpolate into the <code>api.json.get</code> URL</b> &mdash; unlike <code>include</code> (Part&nbsp;2 &sect;C). It used to read <code>/tractionrec/_flat/course/</code> copies; since &sect;0 the real files are readable as they are.</p>
<pre class="run">&#123;&#123;each courses as c sort by c.course_name asc limit 0,3&#125;&#125;
  &lt;b&gt;&#123;&#123;c.course_name&#125;&#125;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;a href="&#123;&#123;c.getUrl()&#125;&#125;"&gt;&#123;&#123;c.getUrl()&#125;&#125;&lt;/a&gt;
  &#123;&#123;each api.json.get(https://kroc.zesty.dev/tractionrec/courses/&#123;c.tractionrec_id&#125;.json) as s&#125;&#125;
    &rarr; TR &lt;code&gt;&#123;&#123;s.Name&#125;&#125;&lt;/code&gt;, program &lt;code&gt;&#123;&#123;s.TREX1__Program__c&#125;&#125;&lt;/code&gt;
  &#123;&#123;end-each&#125;&#125;&lt;br&gt;
&#123;&#123;end-each&#125;&#125;</pre>
  <div class="out"><span class="out-label">Output &mdash; server-rendered, first 3 courses (3 fetches)</span>
  {{each courses as c sort by c.course_name asc limit 0,3}}<b>{{!c.course_name}}</b> <a href="{{c.getUrl()}}">{{c.getUrl()}}</a> {{each api.json.get(https://kroc.zesty.dev/tractionrec/courses/{c.tractionrec_id}.json) as s}}&rarr; TR <code>{{!s.Name}}</code>, program <code>{{!s.TREX1__Program__c}}</code>{{end-each}}<br>{{end-each}}
  </div>
